# 22. Limitations and Open Problems

The RMSS concept is presently a theoretical architecture rather than an experimentally validated general intelligence system.

Several problems remain fundamental.

### 22.1 Mathematical representation

A finite vocabulary cannot contain every mathematical structure.

### 22.2 Relationship learning

It remains an open engineering problem to learn useful high-dimensional relationships without introducing spurious correlations.

### 22.3 Verification

Not every mathematical property is computationally decidable.

### 22.4 Search complexity

The number of possible transformation paths can grow combinatorially.

### 22.5 Generalization

A program that performs well on observed data may fail under distributional change.

### 22.6 Self-modification

Allowing programs to modify their own synthesis mechanisms introduces substantial verification and safety requirements.

### 22.7 Domain assumptions

A mathematically elegant model can still be empirically wrong if its assumptions do not correspond to the environment.

These limitations are not peripheral; they are central research questions for any practical implementation.

