# 24. Conclusion

The Relational Mathematical State Space proposes a computational architecture in which mathematical reasoning is represented as navigation through a structured space of mathematical concepts and transformations.

Its defining components are:

[
\boxed{
\mathcal V_t=
(\mathcal M,\mathcal R_t,\mathcal C,\mathcal P_t,\mathcal H_t)
}
]

with:

[
\mathcal M=\text{mathematical knowledge},
]

[
\mathcal R_t=\text{learned relationships},
]

[
\mathcal C=\text{mathematical validity},
]

[
\mathcal P_t=\text{candidate procedures},
]

and:

[
\mathcal H_t=\text{historical experience}.
]

A problem is embedded into this space, relevant mathematical neighborhoods are activated, candidate transformation paths are synthesized, invalid procedures are eliminated, remaining procedures are optimized, and the selected path is compiled into executable code.

The resulting system forms a feedback loop:

[
\boxed{
\text{Problem}
\rightarrow
\text{Mathematical Representation}
\rightarrow
\text{Transformation Path}
\rightarrow
\text{Verification}
\rightarrow
\text{Optimization}
\rightarrow
\text{Execution}
\rightarrow
\text{Observation}
\rightarrow
\text{Learning}.
}
]

The central hypothesis is therefore:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{
Mathematical intelligence can be modeled as the ability to discover, validate, optimize, and compose relationships between mathematical transformations.
}
}
]

Under this interpretation, executable code is not the fundamental intelligence of the system. It is the compiled representation of a higher-level mathematical procedure.

The proposed architecture consequently occupies an intersection between **symbolic mathematics, program synthesis, dynamical systems, optimization, formal verification, machine learning, and mathematical knowledge representation**.

Its most ambitious implication is that an adaptive computational system could gradually construct an empirical map of the mathematical transformations that are useful for different classes of problems, while retaining a formally constrained substrate that prevents empirical optimization from being confused with mathematical validity.

The RMSS is therefore best regarded not as a claim that a complete autonomous mathematician has already been constructed, but as a proposed architecture for investigating whether **mathematical problem solving can itself be treated as an evolving, constrained computational process**.
